      "id": "deep-study-1",
      "title": "Deep Study Workshop 1: Connecting the Evidence",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Historical reasoning",
          "color": "blue",
          "text": "In Forest Society and Colonialism, historical understanding improves when evidence is connected rather than memorised separately. Start with the setting, then identify the institutions and social groups involved. Next ask what changed, why it changed, who benefited or suffered, and what evidence demonstrates the change. A strong explanation makes the relationship between these elements explicit.\\n\\nWhen revising, turn every major heading into a question. For example: What conditions existed? What problem did people face? What action was taken? Why did the action matter? What were the immediate and long-term consequences? This method turns passive reading into active historical reasoning and makes unfamiliar examination questions easier to solve."
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Model reasoning chain",
          "color": "orange",
          "text": "Context → pressure → decision or conflict → response → consequence → historical significance. The chain should not be treated as a rigid formula; it is a checking tool. If an answer contains only an event but no explanation of why it happened or what it changed, the reasoning is incomplete."
        },
        {
          "type": "mistakes",
          "title": "Common misconceptions",
          "color": "red",
          "text": "Do not assume that a government announcement automatically produced the intended result. Do not treat all members of a social group as identical. Do not confuse a historical image's symbolism with literal evidence of every event. Do not write present-day meanings into a historical term without checking its context."
        },
        {
          "type": "practice",
          "title": "Extended practice",
          "color": "purple",
          "questions": [
            {
              "difficulty": "Basic",
              "question": "Name one major structural condition discussed in Forest Society and Colonialism.",
              "answer": "A structural condition is a long-running political, economic, social or environmental circumstance that shaped later events.",
              "solution": "Choose one condition from the relevant lesson and explain it in one sentence."
            },
            {
              "difficulty": "Understanding",
              "question": "Why should historians distinguish structure from event?",
              "answer": "Because long-term structures help explain why an event became possible, while the event is a particular occurrence in time.",
              "solution": "Give one long-term condition and one immediate event."
            },
            {
              "difficulty": "Application",
              "question": "How would you use a historical image in an answer?",
              "answer": "Identify it, place it in context, describe what it shows, and explain what historical point it supports.",
              "solution": "Never use an image as decoration; connect it to an argument."
            },
            {
              "difficulty": "HOTS",
              "question": "Can a policy be both transformative and harmful? Explain.",
              "answer": "Yes. A policy may create new institutions or opportunities while imposing unequal costs or restrictions on particular groups.",
              "solution": "Give one benefit and one cost, then explain why the judgement depends on perspective and evidence."
            },
            {
              "difficulty": "Challenge",
              "question": "How can historical evidence challenge a simple textbook narrative?",
              "answer": "Different sources may reveal competing experiences, omissions or consequences, allowing historians to test whether a single narrative explains all available evidence.",
              "solution": "Compare an official source with evidence from affected people when possible."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
